WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.100
How does SAT reduce to

2
00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:05.500
exactly-three-literal SAT? In this course

3
00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:07.320
SAT takes a conjunction of

4
00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.800
clauses, while three SAT requires

5
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:12.240
exactly three distinct literals per

6
00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.060
clause. We will add fresh

7
00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:16.400
variables so satisfiability is preserved

8
00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:18.900
under assignment extension, not claim

9
00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:20.200
the new formula has an

10
00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:21.740
identical truth function on the

11
00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:22.780
old variable set.

12
00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:25.800
First remove duplicate literals and

13
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:28.440
discard tautological clauses, and treat

14
00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:30.040
an empty clause as a

15
00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:33.180
fixed unsatisfiable case. These checks

16
00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:35.560
preserve satisfiability and ensure the

17
00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:37.120
remaining short clauses have the

18
00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:40.300
expected distinct original literals. Auxiliary

19
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:41.760
variables must be fresh for

20
00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:43.900
each clause so independently chosen

21
00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:45.520
extensions do not interfere.

22
00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:48.700
For a two-literal clause, introduce

23
00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:50.480
u and make two clauses,

24
00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:52.280
one containing u and one

25
00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:54.540
containing not u. If either

26
00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:56.700
original literal is true, both

27
00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:57.980
new clauses are true for

28
00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:00.340
either u. If both originals

29
00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:01.940
are false, the pair demands

30
00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:03.980
u and not u simultaneously,

31
00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:05.240
which is impossible.

32
00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:07.600
For a one-literal clause x,

33
00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:09.700
introduce u and v and

34
00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:11.220
use all four sign combinations

35
00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:12.880
of those two variables beside

36
00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:14.740
x. When x is true,

37
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:17.140
all four clauses hold. When

38
00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:19.000
x is false, every assignment

39
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:20.420
to u and v falsifies

40
00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:21.540
one of the four clauses,

41
00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:23.280
so there is no satisfying

42
00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:23.900
extension.

43
00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:26.860
A four-literal clause uses one

44
00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:29.380
linking variable. The first new

45
00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:31.060
clause contains its first two

46
00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:32.760
literals and u, while the

47
00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:34.480
second contains not u and

48
00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:36.360
the last two literals. If

49
00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:37.680
the originals are all false,

50
00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:39.540
one clause forces u true

51
00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:40.840
and the other forces it

52
00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:41.380
false.

53
00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:42.980
If an original literal on

54
00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:44.480
the left is true, choose

55
00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:45.860
u false so the right

56
00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:47.420
clause is satisfied by not

57
00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:48.700
u. If a literal on

58
00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:50.240
the right is true, choose

59
00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:51.520
u true so the left

60
00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:53.060
clause is satisfied by u.

61
00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:55.000
This proves the stronger fact

62
00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:56.700
that each satisfying assignment to

63
00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:58.040
the original variables can be

64
00:01:58.040 --> 00:01:59.000
extended to the gadget.

65
00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:01.580
For six literals, chain three

66
00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:03.780
fresh variables through four clauses.

67
00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:06.600
Each intermediate clause passes the

68
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:08.140
obligation to the next link

69
00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:09.640
when its original literal is

70
00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:11.440
false, while the final clause

71
00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:13.360
must eventually be satisfied by

72
00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:15.520
an original literal. A true

73
00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:17.220
original clause lets the linking

74
00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:19.620
choices switch appropriately, whereas all

75
00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:21.880
false literals force a contradiction

76
00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:22.780
along the chain.

77
00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:25.260
In general a k-literal clause

78
00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:26.860
with k greater than three

79
00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:29.120
needs k minus three linking

80
00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:30.620
variables and k minus two

81
00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:32.780
clauses. The four-literal case has

82
00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:34.060
only the first and final

83
00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:36.360
clause, so no nonexistent previous

84
00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:38.460
linking variable is introduced. The

85
00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:39.940
total gadget size is linear

86
00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:41.380
in the original literal occurrences

87
00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:42.460
after normalization.

88
00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:45.080
For the complete formula, keep

89
00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:48.100
each original-variable assignment consistent across

90
00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:50.380
all clause gadgets. If the

91
00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:52.720
original assignment satisfies every clause,

92
00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.220
fresh local auxiliary sets let

93
00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:57.920
all extensions coexist. If the

94
00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:00.760
transformed formula is satisfiable, each

95
00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:02.540
gadget forces its original clause

96
00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:05.000
true, so the restricted assignment

97
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:07.400
satisfies the entire original conjunction.

98
00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:10.840
Showing each clause individually satisfiable

99
00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:12.440
is not enough to prove

100
00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:15.360
the conjunction satisfiable, because clauses

101
00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:18.200
share original variables. The required

102
00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:20.940
statement is original-clause truth under

103
00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:22.880
a fixed assignment if and

104
00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:24.600
only if some local extension

105
00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.620
satisfies its gadget. Preserve every

106
00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:30.260
negation visually, since PDF text

107
00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:32.380
extraction can lose overbars.

108
00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:33.780
Things to take note of.

109
00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.060
Handle clauses of one, two,

110
00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:38.040
three and more literals separately,

111
00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:40.720
introduce fresh auxiliaries, and prove

112
00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:43.300
both extension directions. Use the

113
00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:46.340
course’s exact-three convention, check empty

114
00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:48.580
and tautological cases, and distinguish

115
00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:50.540
conjunctive normal form from the

116
00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:52.200
Chomsky normal form used for

117
00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:52.640
grammars.
